<h1>Pharmaceutical &amp; Cleanroom Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Pharmaceutical conveyors transport tablets, capsules, vials, syringes, and packaging components through cleanroom environments while meeting ISO 14644 classification requirements and cGMP (current Good Manufacturing Practice) regulations. These systems use stainless steel frames (often 316L for product contact), enclosed or HEPA-protected conveyors in the most critical zones, low-particulate drive components, and validated material documentation. Containment conveyors add isolator integration and HEPA-filtered exhaust for handling highly potent compounds (HPAPIs).</p>
<h2 id="iso-heading">ISO Cleanroom Classifications</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.iso.org/standard/53394.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ISO 14644-1</a> defines cleanroom classifications by the maximum allowable particle count per cubic meter of air. Each classification imposes specific requirements on the conveyor equipment operating within that space. The conveyor itself must not generate particles that would violate the room classification, every moving part, belt surface, and lubricated joint is a potential particle source.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>ISO Class</th>
<th>FED STD 209E</th>
<th>Max particles/m3 (≥0.5 µm)</th>
<th>Pharma Application</th>
<th>Common Conveyor Practice</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>ISO 5</td>
<td>Class 100</td>
<td>3,520</td>
<td>Aseptic critical fill zone</td>
<td>Enclosed or HEPA-protected, no exposed lubrication</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ISO 7</td>
<td>Class 10,000</td>
<td>352,000</td>
<td>Area next to the aseptic line, some oral solid dosage</td>
<td>Low-particulate drives, smooth surfaces</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ISO 8</td>
<td>Class 100,000</td>
<td>3,520,000</td>
<td>Less critical support areas, isolator background, some packaging</td>
<td>Standard cleanroom-compatible construction</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CNC</td>
<td>None</td>
<td>Not classified</td>
<td>Secondary packaging, warehouse</td>
<td>Clean design, easy-to-wipe surfaces</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Source note: class equivalents, particle limits, and aseptic uses from <a href="https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/sterile-drug-products-produced-aseptic-processing-current-good-manufacturing-practice" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDA, Sterile Drug Products Produced by Aseptic Processing (2004)</a>. FED STD 209E was superseded by ISO 14644-1, but its class names remain in common use. CNC = Controlled Not Classified. Classes for oral solid dosage and packaging are set by the manufacturer's risk assessment.<!-- VERIFY: OSD and packaging class assignments are typical practice, not regulatory requirements; confirm with a pharma facility design reference before adding specifics. --> The conveyor practice column is common industry practice, not a regulatory requirement.</p>
<h2 id="gmp-heading">cGMP Compliance for Conveyors</h2>
<p>Current Good Manufacturing Practice (cGMP) under FDA 21 CFR Parts 210 and 211 requires that pharmaceutical equipment be designed, constructed, and maintained to facilitate cleaning, prevent contamination, and support process validation. The equipment rules in <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-211/subpart-D" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR 211.63 to 211.67</a> say product-contact surfaces must not be reactive, additive, or absorptive, and lubricants or coolants must not contact the product. For conveyors, cGMP compliance means:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Materials of construction:</strong> 316L stainless steel for product-contact frames. Polymer grades that comply with FDA food-contact regulations in <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-B/part-177" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR Part 177</a> (for example UHMW-PE, acetal, silicone) for belt surfaces and wear strips. Material certificates (mill certs) for all metal components.</li>
<li><strong>Surface finish:</strong> Product-contact surfaces polished to a maximum Ra stated in the user requirement specification (URS). Electropolishing is common for corrosion resistance and cleanability. Similar requirements to <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary food conveyors</a> but with additional documentation requirements.</li>
<li><strong>Documentation package:</strong> Design Qualification (DQ), Factory Acceptance Test (FAT), Installation Qualification (IQ), Operational Qualification (OQ), and Performance Qualification (PQ) protocols. Component traceability, weld maps, and surface finish test reports.</li>
<li><strong>Change control:</strong> Any modification to the conveyor after validation requires a formal change control process. Even belt replacement must follow documented procedures with material verification.</li>
</ul>
<p>The documentation and qualification work is a major cost driver over equivalent industrial conveyors. Ask suppliers to price DQ, FAT, and IQ/OQ support as separate line items so quotes can be compared. For cost factors, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="design-heading">Cleanroom Conveyor Design</h2>
<p>Cleanroom conveyor design focuses on minimizing particle generation, enabling thorough cleaning, and preventing cross-contamination between products. Key design features:</p>
<h3>Particle Control</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Enclosed drives:</strong> Motors and gearboxes mount inside sealed housings. Drive belts or chains run inside enclosed channels. No exposed moving parts that generate or distribute particles.</li>
<li><strong>Low-particulate bearings:</strong> Sealed bearings with clean-grease fill. Self-lubricating polymer bearings eliminate lubricant-generated particles entirely.</li>
<li><strong>Smooth belt surfaces:</strong> Belt materials that do not generate fiber, flake, or dust. PU (polyurethane) belts with smooth, sealed surfaces are standard. No fabric-backed belts that shed fibers.</li>
<li><strong>Minimal fasteners:</strong> Reduce exposed bolt heads, screw threads, and crevices that trap particles and resist cleaning.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Cleaning and Changeover</h3>
<p>Pharmaceutical conveyors must support cleaning between product batches to prevent cross-contamination. Cleaning validation demonstrates that the cleaning procedure removes product residue to below acceptable limits. Conveyor design features that support cleaning validation:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tool-free belt removal:</strong> Belts remove for cleaning or inspection without tools. Quick-release tensioners and lift-up tail designs.</li>
<li><strong>Crevice-free construction:</strong> No dead legs, no hidden cavities, no trapped air spaces where residue accumulates.</li>
<li><strong>Wipeable surfaces:</strong> All external surfaces smooth and wipeable with IPA (isopropyl alcohol) or validated cleaning agents.</li>
<li><strong>WIP (Wash-in-Place):</strong> For contained conveyors handling HPAPIs, integrated spray nozzles clean internal surfaces without opening the containment barrier.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="containment-heading">Containment Conveying for Potent Compounds</h2>
<p>Highly potent active pharmaceutical ingredients (HPAPIs) typically require containment conveying. One common industry definition, cited in <a href="https://www.pharmtech.com/view/examining-high-potency-api-manufacturing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pharmaceutical Technology</a>, treats an API with an occupational exposure limit (OEL) at or below 10 µg/m3 as highly potent. The conveyor operates inside an enclosed system, isolator, glovebox, or containment housing, that prevents operator exposure to airborne drug particles.</p>
<p>Containment conveyor features include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Enclosed housing:</strong> The conveyor runs inside a sealed enclosure with HEPA-filtered exhaust maintaining negative pressure relative to the room.</li>
<li><strong>Gloveport access:</strong> Operators interact with the conveyor through gloveports in the enclosure wall for belt changes, jam clearing, and maintenance.</li>
<li><strong>Split-butterfly valves:</strong> Product transfers into and out of the contained conveyor use split-butterfly valves (SBVs) that maintain containment during connection and disconnection.</li>
<li><strong>WIP/CIP systems:</strong> Integrated wash nozzles clean the conveyor and enclosure interior without breaking containment. Wash water drains to a contained waste system.</li>
<li><strong>Continuous liner systems:</strong> Waste product and cleaning materials discharge through continuous liners that seal contaminated material without exposure.</li>
</ul>
<p>Containment conveyor systems cost significantly more than standard pharmaceutical conveyors. The cost drivers are the enclosure, filtration, transfer valves, wash systems, validation, and containment testing. OEL testing (operator exposure monitoring) during commissioning verifies containment performance.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Pharmaceutical Conveyor Applications</h2>
<h3>Oral Solid Dosage (OSD)</h3>
<p>Tablet and capsule lines use conveyors between the tablet press or capsule filler, metal detector, deduster, coating pan, and packaging. Belt conveyors with smooth polyurethane belts transport loose tablets. Enclosed <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevators</a> or <a href="/conveyor-types/vacuum-conveyors/">vacuum conveyors</a> handle vertical transfers. <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing conveyors</a> position bottles under tablet counters and cappers.</p>
<h3>Aseptic Fill-Finish</h3>
<p>Vial, syringe, and cartridge filling lines operate in ISO Class 5 environments. Conveyors transport containers from the washer through depyrogenation tunnels to fill-finish machines. Pallet transfer (workpiece carrier) conveyors provide the precision positioning needed for robotic filling and stoppering. These conveyors run inside unidirectional (laminar) airflow zones with HEPA-filtered supply. <a href="https://health.ec.europa.eu/system/files/2022-08/20220825_gmp-an1_en_0.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU GMP Annex 1</a> (clause 5.8) says a conveyor belt should not pass through a partition between a Grade A or B area and a lower-grade area unless the belt is continually sterilised. See <a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/cleanroom/">cleanroom conveyors</a> for design details.</p>
<h3>Pharmaceutical Packaging</h3>
<p>Pharmaceutical packaging lines use conveyors between blister packers, cartoners, bundlers, serialization stations, and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/">case packers</a>. Serialization (track-and-trace) requirements add vision inspection stations and <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject conveyors</a> at each serialization point. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation conveyors</a> buffer between machines to maintain line efficiency.</p>
<h2 id="evaluate-heading">What to Ask Pharmaceutical Conveyor Suppliers</h2>
<p>Many general-purpose conveyor builders offer cleanroom or sanitary options. Specialized pharmaceutical builders add validation support. Use these questions to separate them:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Particle data:</strong> Do you have particle emission test results for this model at our speed and load, and which method was used?</li>
<li><strong>Materials:</strong> Can you supply mill certificates for metals and food-contact compliance statements for every polymer in the product zone?</li>
<li><strong>Surface finish:</strong> What Ra do you guarantee on product-contact and non-contact surfaces, and how is it measured?</li>
<li><strong>Cleaning and sterilization:</strong> Which cleaning agents and VHP cycles is the belt rated for, and do you have exposure test data?</li>
<li><strong>Documentation:</strong> Do you provide DQ, FAT, and IQ/OQ protocols, and are they priced separately?</li>
<li><strong>Change control:</strong> How do you document belt or component substitutions after validation?</li>
<li><strong>References:</strong> Can you show installations in the same ISO class and dosage form?</li>
</ul>
<p>See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/">manufacturer directory</a> for supplier listings.</p>
